1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,654
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,713 --> 00:00:09,860
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:10,569
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,628 --> 00:00:13,401
Welcome to Unit 3: Ecosystems.

5
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:20,662
Bees carry pollen from flower to flower. Without pollinators, many plants couldn't make seeds and fruit,

6
00:00:20,662 --> 00:00:24,383
and whole ecosystems, including our food supply, would suffer.

7
00:00:24,383 --> 00:00:29,168
Let's explore how living things depend on each other, and how humans affect them.

8
00:00:30,227 --> 00:00:35,898
A population is a group of organisms of one species living in an area, like a herd of elephants.

9
00:00:36,957 --> 00:00:43,455
A community is all the populations of different species living in the same area: elephants, giraffes, zebras,

10
00:00:43,455 --> 00:00:45,049
trees and grasses together.

11
00:00:46,108 --> 00:00:51,602
An ecosystem is the community plus all its interactions with non-living factors, like light,

12
00:00:51,602 --> 00:00:56,091
water and temperature. Organisms depend on each other for food and shelter.

13
00:00:56,091 --> 00:01:00,935
An ecosystem can be as big as a mountain range, or as small as a sealed glass jar!

14
00:01:01,994 --> 00:01:07,133
There are many types of ecosystem. Forests get lots of rain and have very varied life.

15
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:13,336
Grasslands are open, sunny and windy. Deserts get little rain and lots of sun, with only a few producers.

16
00:01:14,395 --> 00:01:19,120
Freshwater ecosystems include ponds, rivers and lakes and the land around them.

17
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:24,318
Saltwater ecosystems, the oceans, cover most of Earth's surface and include coral reefs.

18
00:01:25,377 --> 00:01:28,036
Within an ecosystem there are many habitats.

19
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:32,170
A habitat is the place where a particular organism lives and thrives.

20
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:37,782
A deer's habitat is the forest, but a tiny beetle's habitat might be the bark of a single tree.

21
00:01:38,841 --> 00:01:42,504
Twelve woodlice are placed in the middle of a choice chamber.

22
00:01:42,504 --> 00:01:46,048
One side is dry and light; the other side is damp and dark.

23
00:01:46,048 --> 00:01:50,773
Predict: where will most of the woodlice be after ten minutes? Pause and decide.

24
00:01:51,832 --> 00:01:55,436
Watch. The woodlice crawl around the chamber for ten minutes.

25
00:01:56,495 --> 00:02:01,516
Ten woodlice are now on the damp, dark side, and only two are on the dry, light side.

26
00:02:02,575 --> 00:02:07,182
Explain what you saw. Woodlice lose water easily through their body covering,

27
00:02:07,182 --> 00:02:11,022
so they survive best in damp places, like under logs and stones.

28
00:02:11,022 --> 00:02:17,519
Moving away from light also helps them hide from predators. This behaviour is an adaptation to their habitat.

29
00:02:17,519 --> 00:02:19,823
Pause and explain it in your own words.

30
00:02:20,882 --> 00:02:28,266
A class places square frames called quadrats at random in three parts of the school field and counts the daisies in each one.

31
00:02:29,325 --> 00:02:35,586
In full sun there are about 14 daisies per quadrat. In part shade there are 8, and under the trees only 2.

32
00:02:36,646 --> 00:02:41,489
Pause and decide: which non-living factor most likely affects where daisies grow,

33
00:02:41,489 --> 00:02:44,384
and why do we use many quadrats placed at random?

34
00:02:45,443 --> 00:02:50,582
Each organism has features that help it survive in its habitat. These are adaptations.

35
00:02:50,582 --> 00:02:55,012
A forest monkey has physical adaptations: long, strong limbs for climbing,

36
00:02:55,012 --> 00:03:00,801
a tail for balance and gripping branches, feet that grip like hands, and teeth that grind leaves,

37
00:03:00,801 --> 00:03:05,763
seeds and fruit. It also has a behavioural adaptation: living high in the treetops,

38
00:03:05,763 --> 00:03:08,953
where there's food and protection from most predators.

39
00:03:10,012 --> 00:03:16,332
A dolphin is adapted to the ocean. It has a streamlined body, a blowhole on top of its head for breathing,

40
00:03:16,332 --> 00:03:21,353
fins and flippers to steer, a strong tail, and a thick layer of blubber to keep warm.

41
00:03:22,412 --> 00:03:28,851
Dolphins also have behavioural adaptations: they live in groups called pods and communicate with each other.

42
00:03:28,851 --> 00:03:30,564
Plants have adaptations too.

43
00:03:30,564 --> 00:03:36,175
The resurrection plant curls up into a ball to survive drought, then opens again when it rains!

44
00:03:37,235 --> 00:03:40,720
Human activities and climate change threaten biodiversity.

45
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:44,914
Scientists estimate that two out of five plant species are endangered,

46
00:03:44,914 --> 00:03:49,226
and losing them could threaten many other organisms and our food supply.

47
00:03:49,226 --> 00:03:54,365
So botanists and geneticists store seeds in more than 1500 seed banks around the world.

48
00:03:55,424 --> 00:04:00,268
Seeds are kept in the dark, with low humidity, at about minus 20 degrees Celsius,

49
00:04:00,268 --> 00:04:01,981
so they won't start to grow.

50
00:04:01,981 --> 00:04:07,061
Vaults like the Svalbard Global Seed Vault are protected from floods and earthquakes,

51
00:04:07,061 --> 00:04:12,377
and every container is clearly labelled with the plant variety and where it was collected.
